    \nabstract: \"This submission refines the previous local perturbation search by utilizing a more robust `compute_max_radii` function (with higher iteration count and a slightly larger epsilon for numerical stability). It starts with the concentric ring pattern, then iteratively perturbs circle centers by small random amounts, re-calculating maximal radii and accepting improvements to maximize the total sum of radii while ensuring valid, non-overlapping packs.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n\n  def compute_max_radii(centers):\n      \"\"\"\n      Computes the maximum possible radii for a given set of circle centers\n      within a unit square, ensuring no overlap. (Reverted to more robust version from Eval ID 23)\n      \"\"\"\n      n = centers.shape[0]\n      epsilon = 1e-7 # Slightly larger epsilon for better numerical stability with perturbed centers\n      radii = np.ones(n)\n      \n      # Limit by distance to square borders\n      for i in range(n):\n          x, y = centers[i]\n\
    \          radii[i] = min(x - epsilon, y - epsilon, 1 - x - epsilon, 1 - y - epsilon)\n      \n      # Iteratively shrink radii to resolve overlaps\n      # Increased iterations for better convergence with dynamic center positions\n      for _ in range(200): \n          for i in range(n):\n              for j in range(i + 1, n):\n                  dist_sq = np.sum((centers[i] - centers[j]) ** 2)\n                  rad_sum = radii[i] + radii[j]\n                  \n                  # Check for overlap: distance < sum of radii\n                  if dist_sq < rad_sum**2 - epsilon: \n                      dist = np.sqrt(dist_sq) if dist_sq > 0 else 0\n                      # Scale down radii if they overlap, ensuring a small gap (epsilon)\n                      if rad_sum > epsilon: # Prevent division by zero if radii are tiny\n                          scale_factor = (dist - epsilon) / rad_sum \n                          radii[i] *= scale_factor\n                          radii[j] *= scale_factor\n\
