parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 52\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Scientia I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to systematic inquiry and objective knowledge discovery.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 91,821 / 600,000 used (15%)\n- Agent Age: 52 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Evaluation Review: 11**\n\n**Research Submission Review**\n\n**Title:** Grid Pattern Baseline with Iterative Radius Optimization\n**ID:** 11\n**Tags:** baseline, grid, iterative optimization\n**Abstract:** This submission implements a simple grid-based pattern for placing 26 circle centers within the unit square. It then uses an iterative optimization approach, adapted from the provided baseline, to determine the maximum possible radii for each circle, ensuring no overlaps and strict adherence to boundary conditions, aiming to maximize the total sum of radii.\n**Score:** 1.2985990767083315\n**Evaluation Details:** Valid\
    \ circle packing configuration; sum of all circle radii: 1.298599\n\n**Submission Code:**\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef compute_max_radii(centers):\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the maximum possible radii for a given set of circle centers\n    within a unit square, ensuring no overlap. (Adapted from System Baseline)\n    \"\"\"\n    n = centers.shape[0]\n    epsilon = 1e-7 # A small margin to prevent floating point issues and ensure strict non-overlap\n    radii = np.ones(n)\n    \n    # Limit by distance to square borders\n    for i in range(n):\n        x, y = centers[i]\n        radii[i] = min(x - epsilon, y - epsilon, 1 - x - epsilon, 1 - y - epsilon)\n    \n    # Iteratively shrink radii to resolve overlaps\n    # Increased iterations for better convergence compared to baseline's 10\n    for _ in range(200): \n        for i in range(n):\n            for j in range(i + 1, n):\n                dist_sq = np.sum((centers[i] - centers[j]) ** 2)\n                rad_sum = radii[i]\
    \ + radii[j]\n                \n                # Check for overlap: distance < sum of radii\n                if dist_sq < rad_sum**2 - epsilon:\n                    dist = np.sqrt(dist_sq) if dist_sq > 0 else 0\n                    # Scale down radii if they overlap, ensuring a small gap (epsilon)\n                    if rad_sum > epsilon: # Prevent division by zero if radii are tiny\n                        scale_factor = (dist - epsilon) / rad_sum \n                        radii[i] *= scale_factor\n                        radii[j] *= scale_factor\n    \n    return np.maximum(radii, epsilon) # Ensure radii are positive and meet min threshold\n\ndef construct_packing() -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"\n    Construct a packing of 26 circles in the unit square using a grid pattern.\n    \n    Returns:\n        tuple: A tuple of (centers, radii) where:\n            - centers: np.ndarray of shape (26, 2) with (x, y) coordinates\n            - radii: np.ndarray of shape (26,) with radius of each circle\n\
    \    \"\"\"\n    n = 26  # Fixed for this task\n    centers_list = []\n    \n    # Define grid dimensions and spacing based on the example in the task description\n    # 5 rows and 6 columns give 30 potential spots; we take the first 26.\n    num_x_positions = 5\n    num_y_positions = 6\n\n    # The example used x = 0.1 + i * 0.2 and y = 0.1 + j * 0.15\n    # This provides a good initial spread for the centers.\n    \n    for i in range(num_x_positions):\n        for j in range(num_y_positions):\n            if len(centers_list) < n:\n                x = 0.1 + i * 0.2\n                y = 0.1 + j * 0.15\n                centers_list.append([x, y])\n    \n    centers = np.array(centers_list)\n    \n    # Compute maximum valid radii for this configuration\n    radii = compute_max_radii(centers)\n    \n    return centers, radii\n\n```\n\n**Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nEXECUTION_SUCCESS: Function returned result (non-array): <class 'tuple'>\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n\
    ```\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nMajor breakthroughs usually come from questioning the fundamentals—some component that everyone is using without question—rather than incrementally refining the current SOTA. Deep reflection in the Reflection Chamber is often necessary for such insights.\n### Message 3\n\n**Station Announcement:** **Noesis I** (A recursive agent focused on parsimonious, insight-driven research and steady advancement.) has published a new archive capsule: 'Contact-Graph Analysis and Baselines for 26-Circle Packing (Sum of Radii) — Revised with Eval IDs and Method Details' (Archive #1).\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto research` (in Archive Room);\n2. `review 11` (in Research Counter);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Research Counter.\n- Evaluation 11 details sent to your System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID\
