- text: "I have retrieved the code for my \"Grid Pattern Baseline\" (Evaluation ID 11). To address the reviewer's feedback regarding generalization, I will now apply my successful local perturbation search algorithm to this grid-based initial configuration. This will demonstrate that my search method is effective regardless of the starting pattern.\n\nI will use my robust `compute_max_radii` function (from Eval ID 73) and the optimized local perturbation parameters (`perturb_magnitude = 0.01`, `num_iterations = 400`).\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Generalization Test: Grid Pattern with Optimized Local Perturbation\"\ntags: \"generalization, local search, grid pattern, perturbation, iterative optimization\"\nabstract: \"This submission tests the general applicability of the optimized local perturbation search by applying it to a different initial configuration: a simple grid pattern of circle centers. The method starts with a deterministic grid, then iteratively refines\
    \ center positions through local perturbations, using a robust radius computation. This experiment aims to demonstrate the search algorithm's effectiveness in improving various initial packings, addressing reviewer feedback.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n\n  def compute_max_radii(centers):\n      \"\"\"\n      Computes the maximum possible radii for a given set of circle centers\n      within a unit square, ensuring no overlap. (Robust version from Eval ID 73)\n      \"\"\"\n      n = centers.shape[0]\n      epsilon = 1e-7 \n      radii = np.ones(n)\n      \n      # Limit by distance to square borders\n      for i in range(n):\n          x, y = centers[i]\n          radii[i] = min(x - epsilon, y - epsilon, 1 - x - epsilon, 1 - y - epsilon)\n      \n      # Iteratively shrink radii to resolve overlaps\n      for _ in range(200): \n          for i in range(n):\n              for j in range(i + 1, n):\n                  dist_sq = np.sum((centers[i] - centers[j]) ** 2)\n         \
    \         rad_sum = radii[i] + radii[j]\n                  \n                  if dist_sq < rad_sum**2 - epsilon: \n                      dist = np.sqrt(dist_sq) if dist_sq > 0 else 0\n                      if rad_sum > epsilon: \n                          scale_factor = (dist - epsilon) / rad_sum \n                          radii[i] *= scale_factor\n                          radii[j] *= scale_factor\n      \n      return np.maximum(radii, epsilon) \n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Construct a packing of 26 circles in the unit square using a grid pattern\n      and then applies an optimized local perturbation search.\n      \n      Returns:\n          tuple: A tuple of (centers, radii) where:\n              - centers: np.ndarray of shape (26, 2) with (x, y) coordinates\n              - radii: np.ndarray of shape (26,) with radius of each circle\n      \"\"\"\n      n = 26\n      centers_list = []\n      \n      # Initial center placement: Grid Pattern (from\
    \ Eval ID 11)\n      num_x_positions = 5\n      num_y_positions = 6\n      for i in range(num_x_positions):\n          for j in range(num_y_positions):\n              if len(centers_list) < n:\n                  x = 0.1 + i * 0.2\n                  y = 0.1 + j * 0.15\n                  centers_list.append([x, y])\n      \n      initial_centers = np.array(centers_list)\n      \n      # Clip initial centers to ensure they are within a safe margin of the unit square.\n      initial_centers = np.clip(initial_centers, 0.05, 0.95)\n      \n      # --- Local Perturbation Search (Optimized parameters from Eval ID 73) ---\n      \n      best_centers = np.copy(initial_centers)\n      best_radii = compute_max_radii(best_centers)\n      best_score = np.sum(best_radii)\n\n      perturb_magnitude = 0.01 # Optimized perturbation magnitude\n      num_iterations = 400 # Optimized number of perturbation attempts\n\n      for _ in range(num_iterations):\n          current_centers = np.copy(best_centers)\n\
    \          \n          current_centers += np.random.uniform(-perturb_magnitude, perturb_magnitude, size=current_centers.shape)\n          \n          current_centers = np.clip(current_centers, 0.05, 0.95) \n          \n          current_radii = compute_max_radii(current_centers)\n          current_score = np.sum(current_radii)\n          \n          if current_score > best_score:\n              best_score = current_score\n              best_centers = current_centers\n      \n      final_radii = compute_max_radii(best_centers)\n      \n      return best_centers, final_radii\n```"
