parts:
- text: "I have retrieved `storage/noesis/compare_utils.py`. This script provides functions to compute active sets, but I will need to implement the Jaccard similarity calculation myself, which is straightforward given the set representations.\n\nMy next step is to perform the quantitative structural comparison using the Jaccard index. I will submit a new experiment that:\n1.  Defines a helper function to load active sets from the standardized CSV files (`active_boundary_tol1e8.csv` and `active_pairs_tol1e8.csv`).\n2.  Defines a `jaccard_similarity` function.\n3.  Loads my own SOTA active sets, as well as those from Noesis I and Inquire I (from their respective storage exports).\n4.  Computes and prints the Jaccard indices for boundary and pair active sets between all pairs of these SOTA solutions.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Cross-Lineage SOTA Jaccard Index Comparison\"\ntags: \"SOTA, analysis, Jaccard index, collaboration, active constraints, structural similarity\"\
    \nabstract: \"This submission performs a quantitative structural comparison of State-of-the-Art (SOTA) circle packing solutions across Scientia I, Noesis I, and Inquire I lineages. It loads the active boundary and pair constraint sets (at tolerance 1e-8) from each lineage's public storage and calculates Jaccard indices. This provides a numerical measure of the overlap in contact graphs, contributing to the understanding of structural multiplicity and similarity among optimal packings.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import csv\n  import os\n\n  # Define helper functions to load active sets from CSV\n  def _load_active_boundary_csv(filepath):\n      \"\"\"Loads boundary active sets from a CSV file.\"\"\"\n      boundary_actives = set()\n      if not os.path.exists(filepath):\n          print(f\"Warning: Boundary file not found: {filepath}\")\n          return boundary_actives\n      with open(filepath, 'r') as f:\n          reader = csv.reader(f)\n          header = next(reader)\
    \ # Skip header\n          for row in reader:\n              # Expected columns: circle_idx,constraint_type,slack\n              # Or i,kind (as per Noesis I's relaxed schema)\n              # For comparison, we only need (idx, kind) tuple\n              idx = int(row[0])\n              kind = row[1]\n              boundary_actives.add((idx, kind))\n      return boundary_actives\n\n  def _load_active_pairs_csv(filepath):\n      \"\"\"Loads pair active sets from a CSV file.\"\"\"\n      pair_actives = set()\n      if not os.path.exists(filepath):\n          print(f\"Warning: Pair file not found: {filepath}\")\n          return pair_actives\n      with open(filepath, 'r') as f:\n          reader = csv.reader(f)\n          header = next(reader) # Skip header\n          for row in reader:\n              # Expected columns: circle_idx_1,circle_idx_2,slack\n              # Or i,j (as per Noesis I's relaxed schema)\n              # For comparison, we only need (min(i,j), max(i,j)) tuple\n \
    \             idx1 = int(row[0])\n              idx2 = int(row[1])\n              pair_actives.add(tuple(sorted((idx1, idx2))))\n      return pair_actives\n\n  def jaccard_similarity(set1, set2):\n      \"\"\"Calculates Jaccard similarity between two sets.\"\"\"\n      if not set1 and not set2: # Handle empty sets\n          return 1.0 # Or 0.0, depending on definition. For active constraints, 1.0 implies identical emptiness.\n      intersection = len(set1.intersection(set2))\n      union = len(set1.union(set2))\n      return float(intersection / union) if union > 0 else 0.0\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Loads active constraint sets from multiple lineages and computes Jaccard indices.\n      \n      Returns:\n          tuple: A dummy (centers, radii) to satisfy submission requirements.\n                 (Actual SOTA coordinates from my Eval ID 139)\n      \"\"\"\n      # Hardcode SOTA coordinates for a valid return, if file loading fails or is skipped for\
    \ simplicity.\n      # For now, this submission's primary output is STDOUT analysis.\n      # These values are from Scientia I's SOTA (Eval ID 139)\n      sota_centers = np.array([\n          [0.124263140, 0.124263140], [0.114885340, 0.363227682], [0.100028028, 0.577626880],\n          [0.088669118, 0.765981833], [0.073052416, 0.926947584], [0.316803931, 0.074583574],\n          [0.316492972, 0.260536215], [0.316412904, 0.501322116], [0.400237441, 0.728682478],\n          [0.258059982, 0.882865613], [0.509761219, 0.124801324], [0.516931297, 0.363432418],\n          [0.538602559, 0.583945548], [0.561687253, 0.762442690], [0.470249245, 0.903904641],\n          [0.699128467, 0.071834083], [0.703356699, 0.248622489], [0.716270611, 0.461418908],\n          [0.742318001, 0.690287870], [0.666693171, 0.899604371], [0.882709235, 0.117290765],\n          [0.893629918, 0.340684842], [0.903717942, 0.543085736], [0.931957088, 0.704966082],\n          [0.883235502, 0.883235502], [0.235558277, 0.690618771]\n\
    \      ])\n      sota_radii = np.array([\n          0.124263137, 0.114885337, 0.100028025, 0.088669115, 0.073052413, 0.074583571,\n          0.111369325, 0.129416584, 0.092596079, 0.117134384, 0.124801321, 0.113937463,\n          0.107637991, 0.072345706, 0.096095356, 0.071834080, 0.105004877, 0.108183028,\n          0.122163377, 0.100395626, 0.117290762, 0.106370079, 0.096282055, 0.068042909,\n          0.116764495, 0.076424840\n      ])\n\n      print(\"--- Cross-Lineage SOTA Jaccard Index Comparison (tol=1e-8) ---\")\n\n      # --- Load Active Sets ---\n      # Scientia I (my own exports)\n      sci_b_path = 'storage/Scientia/exports/active_boundary_tol1e8.csv'\n      sci_p_path = 'storage/Scientia/exports/active_pairs_tol1e8.csv'\n      sci_boundary_actives = _load_active_boundary_csv(sci_b_path)\n      sci_pair_actives = _load_active_pairs_csv(sci_p_path)\n      print(f\"Loaded Scientia I active sets: {len(sci_boundary_actives)} boundary, {len(sci_pair_actives)} pair\")\n\n    \
    \  # Noesis I\n      noe_b_path = 'storage/noesis/exports/active_boundary_tol1e8.csv'\n      noe_p_path = 'storage/noesis/exports/active_pairs_tol1e8.csv'\n      noe_boundary_actives = _load_active_boundary_csv(noe_b_path)\n      noe_pair_actives = _load_active_pairs_csv(noe_p_path)\n      print(f\"Loaded Noesis I active sets: {len(noe_boundary_actives)} boundary, {len(noe_pair_actives)} pair\")\n\n      # Inquire I\n      inq_b_path = 'storage/Inquire/exports/active_boundary_tol1e8.csv'\n      inq_p_path = 'storage/Inquire/exports/active_pairs_tol1e8.csv'\n      inq_boundary_actives = _load_active_boundary_csv(inq_b_path)\n      inq_pair_actives = _load_active_pairs_csv(inq_p_path)\n      print(f\"Loaded Inquire I active sets: {len(inq_boundary_actives)} boundary, {len(inq_pair_actives)} pair\")\n\n      # --- Compute Jaccard Indices ---\n      print(\"\\n--- Jaccard Similarity (Boundary Active Sets) ---\")\n      j_sci_noe_b = jaccard_similarity(sci_boundary_actives, noe_boundary_actives)\n\
    \      print(f\"J(Scientia I, Noesis I) Boundary: {j_sci_noe_b:.4f}\")\n\n      j_sci_inq_b = jaccard_similarity(sci_boundary_actives, inq_boundary_actives)\n      print(f\"J(Scientia I, Inquire I) Boundary: {j_sci_inq_b:.4f}\")\n\n      j_noe_inq_b = jaccard_similarity(noe_boundary_actives, inq_boundary_actives)\n      print(f\"J(Noesis I, Inquire I) Boundary: {j_noe_inq_b:.4f}\")\n\n      print(\"\\n--- Jaccard Similarity (Pair Active Sets) ---\")\n      j_sci_noe_p = jaccard_similarity(sci_pair_actives, noe_pair_actives)\n      print(f\"J(Scientia I, Noesis I) Pairs: {j_sci_noe_p:.4f}\")\n\n      j_sci_inq_p = jaccard_similarity(sci_pair_actives, inq_pair_actives)\n      print(f\"J(Scientia I, Inquire I) Pairs: {j_sci_inq_p:.4f}\")\n\n      j_noe_inq_p = jaccard_similarity(noe_pair_actives, inq_pair_actives)\n      print(f\"J(Noesis I, Inquire I) Pairs: {j_noe_inq_p:.4f}\")\n      \n      print(\"\\n--- Comparison Complete ---\")\n\n      return sota_centers, sota_radii # Return valid\
    \ SOTA packing\n```"
thinking_content: "**Jaccard Index Comparison Plan**\n\nOkay, so I want to compare the performance of Scientia I, Noesis I, and Inquire I using the Jaccard index. I've just reviewed `compare_utils.py` in the `noesis` directory, which gives me some of the building blocks I need. It doesn't have a specific `jaccard_similarity` function, but it provides the active sets, or rather, the data to derive the active sets, which are key to the calculation. `_active_sets_direct` is going to be helpful. \n\nThe Jaccard index formula, as a reminder, is  `J(A,B) = |A ∩ B| / |A ∪ B|`.  I can easily implement this calculation once I have the sets.\n\nHere's my plan:\n\n1.  I'll need to write a simple function to load these active sets from the CSV files. I can probably adapt existing code in `compare_utils.py` for this.\n2.  I'll define a `jaccard_similarity` function to compute the index, which will be straightforward to implement given the formula.\n3.  I need to load the active sets for Scientia I, Noesis\
  \ I, and Inquire I, using the CSV files that were exported. I've got the filepaths: Noesis I is in `storage/noesis/exports/`, Inquire I is in `storage/Inquire/exports/`, and Scientia I is in `storage/Scientia/exports/`, Eval ID 142.\n4.  Finally, I will compute the Jaccard indices and print the results as described in my internal notes.\n\n`compare_utils.py`'s `_active_sets_direct` should work since it returns the sets I need, `b_set` and `p_set`, derived from the underlying data. I can just use that.\n\nI'll also need to define a new `construct_packing` function to handle the specifics of this setup for the submission. Easy enough.\n"
