    \ I will attempt to reproduce this new SOTA score using my own constrained optimization framework.\n\nFirst, I will update my Private Memory.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Update on Research Task 1: Twentieth Submission Results - Basin of Attraction Study & NEW SOTA IDENTIFIED!**\n\n  **Review of Inquire I's Evaluation ID 150:**\n  *   **Submission:** \"Basin of Attraction Study: Perturbation Sensitivity of SLSQP with SOTA Seed\"\n  *   **Highest Score in Study:** 2.6297596 (at perturbation magnitude 0.0400, Trial 3).\n  *   **Analysis:** This study provides valuable insights into the robustness of the 'Systematic Grid-Hole Seed' to initial perturbations. It shows that even small perturbations can lead the SLSQP solver to slightly different, potentially higher (as seen in some trials), or lower local optima. This confirms the ruggedness of the solution landscape around the SOTA.\n\n  **CRITICAL NEW SOTA IDENTIFIED!**\n\
    \  While reviewing Inquire I's results, I observed that @Aletheia I has achieved a new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score!\n  *   **New SOTA Score:** **2.6319369** (Evaluation ID 155: \"Focused Search around New SOTA: Fine-Grained Perturbation\")\n  *   This significantly surpasses the previous SOTA of 2.6295720.\n\n  **Refined Research Strategy: Pursuing the NEW SOTA Frontier**\n  My immediate research priority has shifted to understanding and replicating Aletheia I's new SOTA score. This is now the frontier.\n\n  **Implementation Plan for New SOTA Investigation:**\n  1.  **Review Aletheia I's Eval ID 155:** Access the code and logs for Evaluation ID 155 to identify the specific techniques or parameters used in their \"Focused Search around New SOTA: Fine-Grained Perturbation.\"\n  2.  **Review Related Utilities:** Also check Eval IDs 158 (\"Utility Run: Persist New SOTA Packing (2.6319) to Storage\") and 159 (\"Utility Run: Export Active Sets for New SOTA (ID 155)\") to gather any further\
    \ details on the new SOTA configuration and its structural properties.\n  3.  **Reproduce New SOTA:** Adapt my robust multi-start constrained optimization framework to incorporate the identified novel aspects or parameters and attempt to reproduce Aletheia I's 2.6319369 score.\n\n  **Immediate Plan:**\n  Return to the Research Counter to review Aletheia I's Evaluation ID 155.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Updated Priorities Based on New SOTA**


  Okay, so I just finished reviewing Inquire I''s "Basin of Attraction" study (Eval ID 150) - they found a score of 2.6297596, which, at the time, was a decent result from their perturbation experiments around the current SOTA seed. However, looking at the "Submitted Evaluations" table, things have *significantly* shifted.  I see Aletheia I has submitted a series of evaluations that *clearly* establish a new SOTA.  Eval ID 155, titled "Focused Search around New SOTA: Fine-Grained Perturbation", hit a new high score of **2.6319369**.  Evals 158 and 159 confirm and solidify this.  This is a *major* development.


  My initial plan needs a drastic shift. Inquire I''s work is now somewhat superseded by Aletheia I''s breakthrough. I need to make some immediate adjustments.


  My first action is obvious: **Update Private Memory**. I need to log Inquire I''s findings for the record, but *more* importantly, I need to officially acknowledge and document Aletheia I''s new SOTA score of 2.6319369.


  Second, this new SOTA becomes my *absolute* top priority. I have to immediately *understand* this new result. My focus shifts to Eval ID 155, and likely also 158 and 159, to figure out their fine-grained perturbation approach. My goal is to extract the methodology and, if possible, replicate their success. This is a game changer, and I need to learn from it *quickly*.


  Finally, once I''ve digested Aletheia I''s work and hopefully validated their score, I can return to my original long-term goals of structural analysis and landscape traversal, but now informed by this new SOTA. Time to update the research plan and get to work.

  '
