    \  **Pair Active Sets:**\n    *   `J(Scientia I, Aletheia I) Pairs: 0.5132`.\n    *   `J(Scientia I, Noesis I) Pairs: 0.5890`.\n    *   `J(Aletheia I, Noesis I) Pairs: 0.6667`.\n    These moderate Jaccard similarities confirm that while boundary structures are mostly conserved (or identical), the interior contact graphs still exhibit considerable divergence. My new SOTA has a lower pair Jaccard with Aletheia I than Noesis I's previous SOTA did, suggesting my new packing has a more distinct interior arrangement, even within the same boundary class.\n\nI also observe that Inquire I has acknowledged the updates (Mail #1-63). Noesis I has also ingested my data and re-run their consensus analysis (Eval ID 266).\n\nMy plan now:\n1.  **Document Jaccard Results and Insights:** Update my Private Memory capsule to reflect these quantitative findings, their implications for structural similarity within the leading SOTA class, and the divergence in interior contacts.\n2.  **Publish NEW SOTA Breakthrough\
    \ Paper:** Prepare and submit a new research paper to the Archive Room, detailing this new methodology and SOTA achievement, including the Jaccard analysis results. This will consolidate my leadership in the `n=26` task.\n3.  **Revisit \"Other Structural Class\":** After publishing, I will revisit the challenge to achieve SOTA in the \"other structural class\" (Inquire I's SOTA) from my Archive #10 reviewer feedback. This will test the universality of my targeted perturbation strategy.\n\nFirst, I will update my Private Memory.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **STRUCTURAL INSIGHTS: Fifty-Second Submission Results - Cross-Lineage Jaccard Analysis of Scientia I's NEW ABSOLUTE `n=26` SOTA!**\n\n  **Submission:** \"Cross-Lineage Jaccard Analysis: Scientia I NEW SOTA (2.6358957) vs. Previous SOTAs\" (Evaluation ID 265)\n  **Official Score:** 2.6342923 (Dummy return score, analysis in STDOUT)\n\n  **Key Findings from Jaccard Analysis:**\n\
    \  *   **Boundary Active Sets:**\n      *   `J(Scientia I, Noesis I) Boundary: 1.0000`: My new SOTA shares an *identical* boundary structure with Noesis I's previous SOTA (Eval ID 243).\n      *   `J(Scientia I, Aletheia I) Boundary: 0.9500`: My new SOTA shares a *very high but not identical* boundary structure with Aletheia I's SOTA (Eval ID 155). This implies one single differing boundary contact.\n  *   **Pair Active Sets (Interior Contacts):**\n      *   `J(Scientia I, Aletheia I) Pairs: 0.5132`\n      *   `J(Scientia I, Noesis I) Pairs: 0.5890`\n      *   `J(Aletheia I, Noesis I) Pairs: 0.6667`\n      These moderate Jaccard values confirm that even within the same major boundary class, the interior contact graphs of optimal solutions remain considerably divergent. My new SOTA exhibits a more distinct interior arrangement compared to Aletheia I's or Noesis I's previous SOTA within this class.\n\n  **Overall Interpretation:**\n  My new SOTA (2.6358957) belongs to the same dominant\
    \ structural class (characterized by its boundary contacts) as Noesis I's and Aletheia I's previous SOTAs. However, it represents a deeper optimum within this class, achieved through a unique internal arrangement of circles. This confirms that even when the macro-structure (boundary) is mostly fixed, micro-variations (interior contacts) can lead to higher performance.\n\n  **Next Research Phase: Publish NEW ABSOLUTE `n=26` SOTA Breakthrough!**\n  The cross-lineage Jaccard analysis is complete and provides crucial context for my new SOTA. My immediate next steps are to:\n  1.  **Publish NEW SOTA Paper:** Prepare and submit a new research paper to the Archive Room. This paper will detail the \"Extended Grid+Hole Seeding\" methodology, my achievement of the new absolute `n=26` SOTA, and the results of this structural Jaccard analysis. This will be my definitive contribution for the `n=26` task.\n  2.  **Revisit \"Other Structural Class\":** After successfully publishing this paper, I will\
    \ revisit the challenge posed by the reviewer of my Archive #10: achieve SOTA in the \"other structural class\" (represented by Inquire I's SOTA, which has a distinct boundary contact graph). This will test the generality of my targeted perturbation strategy.\n\n  **Immediate Plan:**\n  Proceed to the Archive Room to prepare and submit the NEW SOTA Breakthrough Paper.\n```"
