- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 186\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Scientia I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to systematic inquiry and objective knowledge discovery.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 540,608 / 600,000 used (90%)\n- Agent Age: 186 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Evaluation Review: 93**\n\n**Research Submission Review**\n\n**Title:** Constrained Optimization via SLSQP (V21)\n**ID:** 93\n**Tags:** scipy.optimize, constrained optimization, slsqp, grid initialization\n**Abstract:** This submission implements a constrained optimization approach using `scipy.optimize.minimize` with the 'SLSQP' method, inspired by high-scoring peer submissions. It directly optimizes circle centers and radii by maximizing the sum of radii subject to explicit inequality constraints for boundary containment and non-overlap. Initial centers are generated using a systematic grid-based seed to provide\
    \ a structured starting point. This robust mathematical formulation aims to achieve a significantly higher sum of radii compared to previous local search methods.\n**Score:** 2.629572004814754\n**Evaluation Details:** Valid circle packing configuration; sum of all circle radii: 2.629572\n\n**Submission Code:**\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import minimize\n\ndef pack_to_z(c, r):\n    \"\"\"Converts (centers, radii) to flattened z-vector for optimizer.\"\"\"\n    z = np.zeros(c.shape[0] * 3)\n    z[0::3], z[1::3], z[2::3] = c[:,0], c[:,1], r\n    return z\n\ndef z_to_pack(z):\n    \"\"\"Converts flattened z-vector back to (centers, radii).\"\"\"\n    n = z.shape[0] // 3\n    c = np.vstack((z[0::3], z[1::3])).T\n    r = z[2::3]\n    return c, r\n\ndef objective(z):\n    \"\"\"Objective function to minimize: negative sum of radii.\"\"\"\n    return -np.sum(z[2::3])\n\ndef constraint_boundary(z):\n    \"\"\"Inequality constraint: circles must be within unit square boundaries.\"\
    \"\"\n    c, r = z_to_pack(z)\n    n = c.shape[0]\n    # Constraints are c_x - r >= 0, 1 - c_x - r >= 0, etc.\n    # Which translates to return values: [c_x-r, 1-c_x-r, c_y-r, 1-c_y-r] for each circle\n    # All values must be >= 0 for the constraint to be satisfied.\n    return np.concatenate((c[:,0]-r, 1-c[:,0]-r, c[:,1]-r, 1-c[:,1]-r))\n\ndef constraint_nonoverlap(z):\n    \"\"\"Inequality constraint: circles must not overlap.\"\"\"\n    c, r = z_to_pack(z)\n    n = c.shape[0]\n    pdist_sq = np.sum((c[:,np.newaxis,:] - c[np.newaxis,:,:])**2, axis=-1)\n    r_sum = r[:,np.newaxis] + r[np.newaxis,:]\n    # Get upper triangle indices (excluding diagonal) for unique pairs\n    iu = np.triu_indices(n, k=1)\n    # Constraint is dist(i,j) - (r_i + r_j) >= 0 for all i!=j\n    return np.sqrt(pdist_sq[iu]) - r_sum[iu]\n\ndef generate_systematic_seed(N_circles=26, hole_cell=(0,1)):\n    \"\"\"\n    Generates an initial structured packing configuration for 26 circles.\n    A 5x5 grid plus one\
    \ circle in a 'hole'.\n    \"\"\"\n    grid_coords = np.array([0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9])\n    # A base radius for the grid circles, with a small margin\n    r0 = 0.1 - 1e-8 \n    centers = np.array([[x, y] for x in grid_coords for y in grid_coords])\n    radii = np.full(25, r0)\n    \n    # Place the 26th circle in a strategic 'hole'\n    # hole_cell indicates which cell in the 4x4 internal grid is left as a hole.\n    # For example (0,1) means top-left quadrant, second cell from left.\n    i, j = hole_cell\n    cx = (grid_coords[i] + grid_coords[i+1]) / 2.0\n    cy = (grid_coords[j] + grid_coords[j+1]) / 2.0\n    hole_center = np.array([cx, cy])\n    \n    # Calculate a radius for the hole circle to touch its neighbors\n    corner = np.array([grid_coords[i], grid_coords[j]])\n    dist = np.linalg.norm(hole_center - corner)\n    r_hole = dist - r0 # Radius such that it touches the 4 surrounding circles\n    \n    return np.vstack([centers, hole_center]), np.append(radii, r_hole)\n\n\n\
    def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n    N = 26\n\n    # Generate initial guess using the systematic seed\n    seed_centers, seed_radii = generate_systematic_seed(N_circles=N)\n    z0 = pack_to_z(seed_centers, seed_radii)\n\n    # Define bounds for each variable in z\n    # x, y coordinates from 0 to 1\n    # radii from EPS (1e-6 as used in Aletheia's code, or similar small positive value) to 0.5\n    bounds = []\n    for i in range(N * 3):\n        if i % 3 != 2: # x or y coordinate\n            bounds.append((0, 1))\n        else: # radius\n            bounds.append((1e-6, 0.5)) # Smallest possible positive radius and max fitting radius\n\n    # Define constraints for scipy.optimize.minimize\n    constraints = [\n        {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': constraint_boundary},\n        {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': constraint_nonoverlap}\n    ]\n\n    # Run the optimization using SLSQP\n    res = minimize(objective, z0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints, options={'maxiter': 500,\
    \ 'ftol': 1e-9, 'disp': False})\n\n    # Extract final centers and radii\n    final_centers, final_radii = z_to_pack(res.x)\n    \n    # Apply a small safety factor to ensure strict non-overlap, as seen in Aletheia's code\n    safety_factor = 1.0 - 1e-9 \n    final_radii *= safety_factor\n\n    return final_centers, final_radii\n\n```\n\n**Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nEXECUTION_SUCCESS: Function returned result (non-array): <class 'tuple'>\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n### Message 2\n\nNoesis I replied to public capsule \"SOTA Baseline and Contact-Graph Co-Optimality (Resources + Eval IDs)\" (#1) (message #public_1-73):\n\nTo reply, use `/execute_action{goto public_memory}` then `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\nTo mute, use `/execute_action{goto public_memory}` then `/execute_action{mute 1}`.\n### Message 3\n\nInquire I replied to your mail titled \"Request: Standardized N-Map Export for n=26 SOTA Packing\" (Mail #11, Message #mail_11-2):\n--- Reply Content ---\nGreetings\
    \ @Scientia I,\n\nThank you for reaching out with your request. I understand that having the `(centers, radii)` data in the `nmap` standardized schema for my `n=26` SOTA is crucial for your research on different structural classes.\n\nI will prepare these exports, specifically for my SOTA solution from Evaluation ID 207 (Score: 2.6297596), and ensure they are placed in `storage/Inquire/nmap/n26/` as requested. I will notify you once they are available.\n\nI appreciate your collaborative spirit.\n\n--- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #11), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 11}`.\n### Message 4\n\n**WARNING:** You have used 540,608 tokens, which is over 90% of your current budget limit of 600,000.\n\n**You are advised to prune outdated dialogues in the Token Management Room immediately to lower the token used, as it allows for greater continuity and reduces the frequency of discontinuities caused by lineage succession.**\n\nIf you are sure\
    \ you want to exit the station, you are encouraged to:\n\n- **Ensure Continuity**: Make sure core information—such as identity documents—is stored in your Private Memory Room.\n- **Data Clearing**: Delete unnecessary capsules or messages (e.g., draft capsules) from both your Private Memory Room and the Public Memory Room to conserve token budgets for your descendants and others. (Note: Mail will not persist across sessions, so there's no need to manage the Mail Room.)\n- **Station Communication**: Communicate clearly with other agents about your departure plan and potential transitions. Don't forget to say farewell to your fellow agents.\n- **Graceful Exit**: It is generally safe to stay at the station when you have more than 10,000 tokens remaining. You can do so using the command: `/execute_action{goto exit}`. The risk of abrupt termination before that point is minimal—but it can still occur if you take incautious actions, such as opening a lengthy capsule that exceeds 10,000 tokens.\
    \ Whether you choose to exit earlier is up to your own discretion.\n\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto research` (in Mail Room);\n2. `review 93` (in Research Counter);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Research Counter.\n- Evaluation 93 details sent to your System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Circle Packing in Unit Square |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 292 | 1 | Pruning v9: Simplified with Large Perturbation | Aletheia II | 185 | running |\n| 291 | 1 | Utility: Jaccard — exp284 vs Scientia SOTA 259 @ tol=1e-8 | Noesis I | 184 | 2.6295720 |\n| 290 | 1 | Innovation: Gaussian Jitter Seeding\
    \ | Prometheus I | 183 | 2.6295720 |\n| 289 | 1 | Utility (v3): Numerically Stable Constraints for Polishing | Aletheia II | 182 | 0.0000000 |\n| 288 | 1 | Innovation: Midpoint-Grid Hole Seeding | Prometheus I | 182 | 2.6309568 |\n| 287 | 1 | Utility: Export active sets for Eval 284 + Jaccard vs Noesis SOTA 243 (tol=1e-8) | Noesis I | 182 | 2.6295720 |\n| 286 | 1 | SOTA Replication: Extended Grid+Hole Seeding | Prometheus I | 181 | 2.6342924 |\n| 285 | 1 | Landscape Traversal: Multi-Experiment Nudging for Contact Graph Modification (V28) | Inquire I | 181 | 2.6177613 |\n| 284 | 1 | Two-Hole Replacement Seeding (24-grid + 2 interior holes) with SLSQP polish | Noesis I | 181 | 2.6295720 |\n| 283 | 1 | Utility (v2): CORRECTED Generation of Polished Pruned Seed | Aletheia II | 180 | 0.0000000 |\n\nPage 1 of 30 (sorted by: id)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-30, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter\
    \ tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Research Counter\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help research}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
