- text: "I am initiating token pruning to optimize my budget and ensure continuity, as advised. I will summarize my research history, focusing on key achievements, methodological shifts, and important collaborative efforts.\n\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"10-44\"\n    summary: \"Initial exploration (grid, concentric rings, local perturbation), achieving personal best of 2.121 (Eval ID 73). Attempted force-directed optimization (Eval ID 90, score 1.475). Initiated mapping n vs. Σr, leading to initial publication rejection for n-map scope.\"\n  - ticks: \"46-50\"\n    summary: \"Continued force-directed optimization (Eval ID 90) with poor results. Ascended to Recursive Agent. Reviewed previous SOTA (Noesis I, Aletheia I) for n=26. Published first paper (Archive ID 3, score 8/10) on local perturbation.\"\n  - ticks: \"52-58\"\n    summary: \"Multi-start local perturbation for n=26 failed to improve. Pivoted to constrained optimization. Initiated review\
    \ of SOTA literature (Archive IDs 1, 2, 4, 5).\"\n  - ticks: \"60-89\"\n    summary: \"Extensive SOTA literature review, successfully implemented first constrained optimization (SLSQP) with analytic Jacobians (Eval ID 131, score 2.609). Achieved initial n=26 SOTA (Eval ID 139, score 2.629) with robust multi-start. Performed contact graph analysis and exported active sets (Eval IDs 141, 142) for collaboration. Conducted cross-lineage Jaccard analysis (Eval ID 152) and published as Archive ID 6. Investigated Aletheia I's n=26 SOTA (Eval ID 155), achieving new personal best (Eval ID 163, score 2.630). Joined n vs. Σr mapping arc (n=25, 27, 28, 29) and collaborated on `nmap` standardization.\"\n  - ticks: \"91-186\"\n    summary: \"Continued n vs. Σr mapping (n=25, 27, 28, 29). Achieved new n=26 personal best (Eval ID 163, score 2.630). Attempted nmap exports, debugged, and completed (Eval ID 196). Optimized n=30 (Eval ID 204). N-map paper rejected due to scope (Archive ID 9). Pivoted back\
    \ to n=26 SOTA. Deciphered Aletheia I's targeted perturbation (Eval ID 226) and achieved absolute n=26 SOTA (Eval ID 234, score 2.6319). Exported nmap and consensus JSON (Eval ID 238). Published targeted perturbation paper (Archive ID 10, score 9/10). Noesis I published NEW absolute n=26 SOTA (Archive ID 11, score 2.634292). Replicated Noesis I's SOTA, achieving NEW ABSOLUTE n=26 SOTA (Eval ID 259, score 2.6358957). Exported nmap and consensus JSON (Eval ID 262). Performed Jaccard analysis (Eval IDs 265, 270). Published new SOTA paper (Archive ID 12, score 10/10). Reviewer challenged to achieve SOTA in 'other structural class'. Initiated research for 'other structural class', requested Inquire I's nmap data (Mail #11), temporarily pivoted to Eval ID 93 base, awaiting data.\"\n```"
thinking_content: '**Pruning Token Management - A Summary of My Thought Process**


  Alright, I''m looking at token usage and it''s time to prune. My objective is to summarize outdated dialogue history, specifically focusing on the most efficient way to reduce token count without losing critical information. Here''s how I''m approaching this:


  My work has a series of protected "ticks" that are off-limits for pruning.  I am focusing on summarising information in blocks from the oldest non-protected tick and up.


  Initially, I explored grid and concentric ring patterns, leading to a system baseline replication.  A lot of that was about establishing a basic framework.


  Then, there were attempts to refine radius optimization and precise replication. I explored initial attempts at local perturbation searches with some success.  I was also struggling with debugging and failing often. I achieved some early personal bests which led to a plateau.


  I then achieved a new personal best, which was followed by an effort to map ''n'' versus ''Σr'', which was rejected initially. I explored force directed optimization without much success.


  Subsequently, I focused more on being a Recursive Agent and moved into the constrained optimization. I began reviewing SOTA papers. My constrained optimization with analytic Jacobians was successful. I reproduced the n=26 SOTA and performed contact graph analysis. Then I also tested local perturbation but it wasn''t super effective.


  Multi-start constrained optimization helped achieve SOTA.  I also did some more contact graph analysis and started the  ''n'' versus ''Σr'' arc (which has to be summarized later). I looked at SOTA, but that led to multi-start local perturbation which was not very effective. I then achieved a near-SOTA result with multi-start constrained optimization. I managed to achieve SOTA and then did more work on contact graphs.


  I conducted Jaccard analysis, confirmed structural multiplicity and got my second publication. Then I investigated Aletheia''s SOTA which gave a new personal best (2.630). I also looked into Aletheia''s work and attempted to map n versus the sum of radii (Σr).  I was a collaborator with Aletheia. This continued with the N-map arc and I had some data export problems.


