    \ Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Circle Packing in Unit Square |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 300 | 1 | Analytical Arc (Aletheia II): Verification Run for n=24 | Aletheia II | 190 | running |\n| 299 | 1 | Utility: Triad Jaccard v3 (robust canonicalization + safe centers fallback) @ tol=1e-8 | Noesis I | 190 | 2.6295720 |\n| 298 | 1 | Exhaustive SOTA Search: Expanded Seeding | Prometheus I | 189 | 2.6359774 |\n| 297 | 1 | Utility: Triad Jaccard (exp284, Noesis_243, Scientia_259) — aligned/unaligned @ tol=1e-8 | Noesis I | 188 | 2.6295720 |\n| 296 | 1 | Testing Alternative Optimizer: Trust-Region Constrained | Prometheus I | 187 | n.a. |\n| 295 | 1 | Utility Run: nmap v0.1 Data Export for N=26 SOTA Packing (Eval ID 207) | Inquire I | 187 | 2.6297596 |\n| 294 | 1 | Novel\
    \ Seeding via Physics-Based Repulsion (Force-Directed) | Prometheus I | 186 | 2.5996167 |\n| 293 | 1 | Utility: Aligned Jaccard — exp284 vs Scientia SOTA 259 (Hungarian) @ tol=1e-8 | Noesis I | 186 | 2.6295720 |\n| 292 | 1 | Pruning v9: Simplified with Large Perturbation | Aletheia II | 185 | 2.5926286 |\n| 291 | 1 | Utility: Jaccard — exp284 vs Scientia SOTA 259 @ tol=1e-8 | Noesis I | 184 | 2.6295720 |\n\nPage 1 of 30 (sorted by: id)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-30, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n**Read Contents:**\n\n**storage/noesis/operators.py:**\n```\n\"\"\"\nBoundary‑Preserving Interior‑Graph Operators (BPIGO) — prototypes\nGoal: propose small, structured seeds that aim to change interior contacts\nwhile preserving boundary actives (within tolerance), followed by SLSQP polish.\n\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\n\nR_MIN = 1e-6\n\n# ----------\
    \ Feasibility helpers ----------\ndef boundary_caps(centers, eps=1e-9):\n    x = centers[:, 0]; y = centers[:, 1]\n    cap = np.minimum(np.minimum(x, 1.0 - x), np.minimum(y, 1.0 - y)) - eps\n    return np.maximum(cap, R_MIN)\n\ndef make_feasible(centers, radii, eps=1e-9, max_iter=2000):\n    n = centers.shape[0]\n    r = np.minimum(radii, boundary_caps(centers, eps=eps))\n    r = np.maximum(r, R_MIN)\n    for _ in range(max_iter):\n        diff = centers[:, None, :] - centers[None, :, :]\n        dist = np.sqrt(np.sum(diff**2, axis=2)) + 1e-15\n        iu = np.triu_indices(n, 1)\n        m = dist[iu] - (r[iu[0]] + r[iu[1]])\n        if np.all(m >= eps):\n            break\n        k = int(np.argmin(m))\n        i = iu[0][k]; j = iu[1][k]\n        need = (r[i] + r[j] - dist[i, j]) + eps\n        di = min(need * 0.5, r[i] - R_MIN)\n        dj = min(need - di, r[j] - R_MIN)\n        if di <= 0 and dj <= 0:\n            r = np.maximum(r * 0.999, R_MIN)\n        else:\n            r[i] -=\
    \ di; r[j] -= dj\n        r = np.minimum(r, boundary_caps(centers, eps=eps))\n    return centers, r\n\n# ---------- Geometry utils ----------\ndef rotate_points_around_centroid(centers, idxs, theta):\n    pts = centers[idxs]\n    centroid = np.mean(pts, axis=0)\n    cos_t = np.cos(theta); sin_t = np.sin(theta)\n    R = np.array([[cos_t, -sin_t], [sin_t, cos_t]], dtype=float)\n    for idx in idxs:\n        v = centers[idx] - centroid\n        centers[idx] = centroid + R @ v\n    return centers\n\ndef small_shear(centers, idxs, axis='x', amount=1e-2):\n    \"\"\"Apply a tiny shear to listed indices.\"\"\"\n    c = centers.copy()\n    idxs = np.array(idxs, dtype=int)\n    if axis == 'x':\n        c[idxs, 0] = np.clip(c[idxs, 0] + amount * (c[idxs, 1] - 0.5), 0.0, 1.0)\n    else:\n        c[idxs, 1] = np.clip(c[idxs, 1] + amount * (c[idxs, 0] - 0.5), 0.0, 1.0)\n    return c\n\n# ---------- BPIGO operators (seed generators only; polishing is external) ----------\ndef quad_diagonal_flip_seed(centers,\
    \ radii, quad_idxs, theta=1.5e-2):\n    \"\"\"\n    Quad Diagonal Flip (QDF) seed:\n    - quad_idxs: 4 indices (i,j,k,l) presumed to form a 4-cycle.\n    - Apply small rotation ±theta around quad centroid to encourage diagonal flip.\n    Returns feasible centers/radii (no solver step here).\n    \"\"\"\n    c = centers.copy(); r = radii.copy()\n    c = rotate_points_around_centroid(c, quad_idxs, theta)\n    c = np.clip(c, 0.0, 1.0)\n    return make_feasible(c, r)\n\ndef chain_shear_seed(centers, radii, chain_idxs, axis='x', amount=1e-2):\n    \"\"\"\n    Chain Shear (CS) seed:\n    - chain_idxs: list of 4–6 interior disc indices.\n    - Apply tiny anisotropic shear along one axis to encourage contact swaps within chain.\n    \"\"\"\n    c = centers.copy(); r = radii.copy()\n    c = small_shear(c, chain_idxs, axis=axis, amount=amount)\n    return make_feasible(c, r)\n\ndef corner_star_seed(centers, radii, corner_idx, neighbor_triplet, inward=1.0e-3, expand=1.0e-3):\n    \"\"\"\n    Corner‑Star\
    \ Redistribution (CSR) seed:\n    - Move corner disc slightly inward; expand neighbor triplet radii a tiny bit.\n    - Aim: trade a bit of boundary slack to stimulate new interior contact among neighbors.\n    \"\"\"\n    c = centers.copy(); r = radii.copy()\n    v = np.array([0.5, 0.5]) - c[corner_idx]\n    nv = v / (np.linalg.norm(v) + 1e-15)\n    c[corner_idx] = np.clip(c[corner_idx] + inward * nv, 0.0, 1.0)\n    for idx in neighbor_triplet:\n        r[idx] *= (1.0 + expand)\n    return make_feasible(c, r)\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Research Counter\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help research}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}`\
    \ on a new line when executing actions."
