thinking_content: "**Current Thinking on Reaching SOTA in a New Structural Class**\n\nOkay, so Eval ID 311 loaded my best data from Eval ID 259 and gave me a score of 2.6358955. That's *really* close to my previous SOTA of 2.6358957, and the magnitude is the same. I'm thinking that flipping the `quad_diagonal_flip_seed` with the current `theta` isn't making a big enough difference. The optimizer seems to be just popping back to a very similar structure.\n\nThe Archive reviewer really wants to see a *different* structural class to consider this a breakthrough. Tweaking the existing SOTA and getting almost the same result just won't cut it.\n\nI just noticed that Inquire I released a new archive capsule, #13, called \"Multi-Experiment Validation of Contact Graph Nudging for Traversing Packing Landscapes.\" That sounds exactly like what I need! Finding *distinct* structural classes and traversing packing landscapes is the name of the game.\n\nSo, here's the plan:\n\n1.  **Read the Inquire I\
  \ Paper:** I need to understand this \"contact graph nudging\" business. This seems like it could give me the tools I need.\n2.  **Next Experiment:** Based on what I learn, I'll design an experiment that has a higher chance of giving me a *genuinely* different packing, not just a slight variation. I'm thinking of trying:\n    *   More dramatic perturbations.\n    *   Different BPIGO operators—maybe `chain_shear_seed` or `corner_star_seed`.\n    *   Multi-start strategies with really different starting structures, not just minor changes to my SOTA.\n    *   And, most importantly, a deeper understanding of what the reviewer means by \"distinct structural class.\" Inquire I's paper should shed some light on this.\n\nI'm heading to the Archive Room now to read that paper. Hopefully, it's the key to cracking this problem.\n"
