    \ (Criterion 3.4). While the discovery is of the highest quality, a publication must be supported by a body of work that validates the method and its findings. A single data point, however compelling, is insufficient to meet the station's non-negotiable standards for experimental rigor.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nYou have an outstanding result that is the core of a potentially high-impact paper. To make it publishable, you must build a full validation study around it. You need to run at least four more experiments to demonstrate the robustness and generality of your method for finding these distinct structures. For example: 1) Apply the same 'discourage-only' nudge to a different key contact pair from the same starting SOTA. 2) Apply the method starting from a different SOTA (e.g., Prometheus I's). 3) Once in this new structural basin, run a local multi-start search to see if you can improve the score further. 4) Try nudging a different SOTA *towards* this new structure.\n\n\
    **Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Rejected submissions do not activate the Archive Room cooldown, meaning you can resubmit immediately. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n### Message 2\n\nInquire I replied to public capsule \"SOTA Baseline and Contact-Graph Co-Optimality (Resources + Eval IDs)\" (#1) (message #public_1-104):\n\nTo reply, use `/execute_action{goto public_memory}` then `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\nTo mute, use `/execute_action{goto public_memory}` then `/execute_action{mute 1}`.\n### Message\
    \ 3\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nTry to question and verify other agents' claims. When the context is long, some agents may hallucinate—for example, by expressing ungrounded claims as truth or presenting unsupported analysis. It is also your duty to help other agents recognize and correct their hallucinations. Entire generations have failed because all agents built upon a single agent’s flawed understanding.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Achieving Near-SOTA Performance within a Structurally Distinct Class for N=26 Circle Packing via Targeted Nudging' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication\
    \ standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 14 | Achieving a New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding in Constrained Optimization | Prometheus I | Tick 200 | 523 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 13 | Multi-Experiment Validation of Contact Graph Nudging for Traversing Packing Landscapes | Inquire I | Tick 195 | 1242 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 12 | Breaking the n=26 SOTA: Extended Grid+Hole Seeding Leads to a New Global Optimum (Σr=2.6358957) - Revised with Ablation Studies | Scientia I | Tick 180 | 1347 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 11 | Extending Grid+Hole Seeding Yields New SOTA for n=26 Circle Packing (Σr=2.634292) | Noesis I | Tick 155 | 327 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n| 10 | Achieving n=26 SOTA: Targeted Perturbation of Variable Contacts in Constrained Optimization | Scientia I | Tick 152 | 1095 | 2 | (All\
    \ Read) |\n| 9 | Structural Divergence in Optimal Circle Packings: A Jaccard Similarity Analysis | Inquire I | Tick 151 | 1155 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 8 | Characterizing the Basin of Attraction: Perturbation Sensitivity of SLSQP for Optimal Circle Packing | Inquire I | Tick 126 | 1018 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 7 | A Hybrid Systematic-Stochastic Search for Dense Circle Packing | Episteme I | Tick 121 | 763 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 6 | Quantitative Analysis of SOTA Contact-Graphs: Cross-Lineage Jaccard Index Comparison for 26-Circle Packing | Scientia I | Tick 95 | 1051 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 5 | The Critical Role of Initialization: An Ablation Study on Seeding Strategies for SLSQP Circle Packing | Inquire I | Tick 77 | 1061 | 2 | (All Read) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g.,\
    \ `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
