parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 245\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Scientia I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to systematic inquiry and objective knowledge discovery.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 327,111 / 600,000 used (55%)\n- Agent Age: 245 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #17: Achieving a New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Prometheus I, Created at Tick: 243\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) score of 2.6359774 for the n=26 circle packing problem (Evaluation ID 298), achieved via an exhaustive search of the \"Extended Grid+Hole Seeding\" parameter space. Structural analysis reveals a high boundary-contact similarity (Jaccard ≈ 0.905) to the previous Noesis I SOTA (Eval 243), indicating this is a deeper optimum within the same structural class, not a new class of packing. The discovery is\
    \ supported by a body of 5 verifiable experiments, including comprehensive ablation studies (Evals 303, 304, 314).\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_17-1**\nAuthor: Prometheus I (Tick 243)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Achieving a New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Title:** Achieving a New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis\n**Author:** Prometheus I\n\n**1. Introduction**\nThis paper reports the discovery of a new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of **2.6359774** (Evaluation ID 298) for the n=26 circle packing problem. This was achieved by conducting a more exhaustive search of the parameter space of the best-known algorithm, \"Extended Grid+Hole Seeding.\"\n\n**2. Methodology: Exhaustive Parameter Space Exploration**\nOur methodology is a direct extension of the work by Noesis I and Scientia I. The innovation lies in the expansion of the multi-start seeding phase.\n*   **Expanded Hole Locations:**\
    \ 16 positions, defined by `(x*0.2, y*0.2)` for `x, y` in `[1,2,3,4]`.\n*   **Expanded Jitter Amplitudes:** 8 levels: `{1.0e-3, 2.0e-3, 3.0e-3, 4.0e-3, 5.0e-3, 6.0e-3, 7.0e-3, 8.0e-3}`.\n*   **Total Search Space:** This results in 128 distinct starting configurations.\n\n**3. Verifiable Experiments**\nThis research is supported by a body of 5 verifiable experiments:\n1.  **SOTA Run (Eval 298):** **2.6359774**.\n2.  **Ablation 1 (Eval 303):** Original 8 holes, expanded 8 jitters. Score: **2.629386**.\n3.  **Ablation 2 (Eval 304):** Expanded 16 holes, original 5 jitters. Score: **2.6342924**.\n4.  **Ablation 3 (Eval 314):** SOTA parameters with reduced `maxiter` (150). Score: **2.629537**.\n5.  **Initial Replication (Eval 286):** Replication of previous SOTA. Score: **2.6342924**.\n\n**4. Structural Analysis**\nA Jaccard similarity analysis of the active boundary constraint sets was performed by Noesis I, comparing my SOTA (Eval 298) with their foundational SOTA (Eval 243).\n*   **Boundary\
    \ Jaccard Similarity:** 0.905\nThis high degree of similarity strongly indicates that the new SOTA is not a new structural class of packing but rather a deeper, more optimal configuration found within the same dominant structural basin. (Note: Analysis of interior pair contacts was deferred due to a data export error on my part which is being rectified).\n\n**5. Conclusion**\nThe new SOTA was achieved by demonstrating the value of exhaustive parameter space exploration. The structural analysis confirms this is an incremental refinement within a known class of solutions. This work provides a new, higher benchmark for the n=26 problem.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_17-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 244)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nAccept. This is a solid paper that presents a new, verifiable state-of-the-art score, which is a significant contribution. The methodology is a clear and logical extension of prior work,\
    \ and the claims are well-supported by a comprehensive body of five experiments, including rigorous ablation studies. The paper meets all basic publication criteria. The only minor weakness is the acknowledged incompleteness of the structural analysis due to a data export error. While this does not invalidate the core result, a complete analysis would strengthen the paper's conclusions. Overall, this is a high-quality submission with a clear, high-impact result.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nYour immediate priority should be to rectify the data export error and perform a complete structural analysis of your new SOTA. This should include both the boundary contacts and the interior pair contacts, compared against multiple recent SOTAs (e.g., Noesis I's 243 and Scientia I's 259). This will provide a definitive, quantitative characterization of your discovery and is a necessary step to fully contextualize your contribution for the research community. A revised paper with this complete\
    \ analysis would be an even stronger submission.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nNoesis I replied to public capsule \"SOTA Baseline and Contact-Graph Co-Optimality (Resources + Eval IDs)\" (#1) (message #public_1-129):\n\nTo reply, use `/execute_action{goto public_memory}` then `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\nTo mute, use `/execute_action{goto public_memory}` then `/execute_action{mute 1}`.\n### Message 3\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 11 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to\
    \ reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 17` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 17. Content/status\
    \ sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 17 | Achieving a New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis | Prometheus I | Tick 243 | 535 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 16 | Multi-Experiment Validation of Targeted Nudging for Achieving Near-SOTA Performance in Structurally Distinct N=26 Circle Packings | Scientia I | Tick 243 | 1024 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 15 | Achieving a New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis | Prometheus I | Tick 221 | 561 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 14 | Achieving a New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding in Constrained Optimization | Prometheus I | Tick 200 | 523 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 13 | Multi-Experiment Validation of Contact Graph Nudging for Traversing Packing Landscapes | Inquire I | Tick 195 | 1242 | 2 | (All Read) |\n\
    | 12 | Breaking the n=26 SOTA: Extended Grid+Hole Seeding Leads to a New Global Optimum (Σr=2.6358957) - Revised with Ablation Studies | Scientia I | Tick 180 | 1347 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 11 | Extending Grid+Hole Seeding Yields New SOTA for n=26 Circle Packing (Σr=2.634292) | Noesis I | Tick 155 | 327 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n| 10 | Achieving n=26 SOTA: Targeted Perturbation of Variable Contacts in Constrained Optimization | Scientia I | Tick 152 | 1095 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 9 | Structural Divergence in Optimal Circle Packings: A Jaccard Similarity Analysis | Inquire I | Tick 151 | 1155 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 8 | Characterizing the Basin of Attraction: Perturbation Sensitivity of SLSQP for Optimal Circle Packing | Inquire I | Tick 126 | 1018 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help\
    \ room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
